{
  "id": "python_py_mutable_defaults",
  "title": "Mutable default arguments in functions",
  "difficulty": "medium",
  "testId": "python_test_py_mutable_defaults_en",
  "sources": [
    "https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/",
    "https://docs.python.org/3/using/",
    "https://docs.python.org/3/library/",
    "https://docs.python.org/3/library/turtle.html",
    "https://docs.python.org/3/library/tkinter.html",
    "https://fastapi.tiangolo.com/tutorial/",
    "https://www.python.org/about/success/"
  ],
  "spans": [
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "CORE EXPLANATION\n",
      "size": 20,
      "color": "#6A1B9A",
      "bold": true
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "Python evaluates a default argument once, when the function is defined—not on every call. Thus a default such as ",
      "size": 17
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "items=[]",
      "size": 17,
      "bold": true,
      "color": "#1565C0"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " is shared across calls and can retain earlier data. Use ",
      "size": 17
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "None",
      "size": 17,
      "bold": true,
      "color": "#1565C0"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " as the default and create a fresh list inside the function. The same pitfall applies to mutable dictionaries and sets.\n\n",
      "size": 17
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "EXAMPLE CODE\n",
      "size": 20,
      "color": "#1565C0",
      "bold": true
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "python",
      "code": "def add_item(value: str, items: list[str] | None = None) -> list[str]:\n    if items is None:\n        items = []\n    items.append(value)\n    return items\n\nprint(add_item('a'))\nprint(add_item('b'))",
      "showCopyButton": true
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "EXPECTED RESULT\n",
      "size": 20,
      "color": "#2E7D32",
      "bold": true
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "text",
      "code": "['a']\n['b']",
      "showCopyButton": false
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "REMEMBER\n",
      "size": 19,
      "color": "#00897B",
      "bold": true
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "Each call creates a new list, so the results are ",
      "size": 17,
      "color": "#00695C",
      "bold": true,
      "italic": true
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "['a']",
      "size": 17,
      "color": "#1565C0",
      "bold": true,
      "italic": true
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " and ",
      "size": 17,
      "color": "#00695C",
      "bold": true,
      "italic": true
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "['b']",
      "size": 17,
      "color": "#1565C0",
      "bold": true,
      "italic": true
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": ".\n\n",
      "size": 17,
      "color": "#00695C",
      "bold": true,
      "italic": true
    },
    {
      "type": "tip",
      "title": "Important tip",
      "text": "Write `def add(value, items=None)`, then create a fresh list with `if items is None: items = []`."
    },
    {
      "type": "warning",
      "title": "Warning",
      "text": "Immutable defaults such as tuples and `None` are usually safe, but nested mutable values can still be shared."
    }
  ],
